infographic list-column-simple-vertical-arrow
data
  title The data-access spectrum: four points, four failure modes
  desc The further a layer drifts from SQL you can see, the more its failures become semantic surprises that execute cleanly — and the later they are caught
  lists
    - label Raw database/sql — checked never
      desc Maximal control, maximal boilerplate. Positional Scan is a standing hazard: reorder two same-typed columns in the SELECT and name lands in email with no error anywhere. A corruption bug, not a crash.
    - label Query builders / squirrel — checked at runtime
      desc The only sound answer for genuinely dynamic queries — twelve optional admin filters. But the query exists only at runtime, so nothing validates it before execution.
    - label Full ORM / GORM — checked never, surprises at runtime
      desc Maximal ergonomics, maximal semantic distance: struct Updates skips zero-valued fields, so a deactivation silently no-ops, and the SQL that runs is never in the diff reviewers see.
    - label sqlc — checked at generate time, in CI
      desc Real SQL files in, typed Go out. Unknown columns, type mismatches and arity errors fail sqlc generate before review; the generated Querier is a free test seam. Its hard limit: one static statement per query.
theme
  palette #6366f1 #f59e0b #8b5cf6 #14b8a6
